# AI Bootstrap Protocol: Machine-Oriented Entry

Spec: AIBootstrap
Purpose:
  The machine-oriented bootstrap protocol — the exact sequence AI follows
  when entering a .sdd project. This is the entry point that ensures
  deterministic, minimum-sufficient context loading without scanning
  the entire .sdd/ directory blindly.

  AI does NOT read entire .sdd/. AI resolves context through the
  Context Compiler using index-first, progressive disclosure.

Owns: bootstrap/AI_BOOTSTRAP.sdd

## Protocol Name

  AI_BOOTSTRAP.v1

## Phase 1: WHO AM I?

  Read: .sdd/bootstrap/identity.sdd

  Purpose: Resolve AI identity — project, environment, role, capabilities.
  Outcome: AI knows its position within the project.

## Phase 2: AUTHORITY CHECK

  Read: .sdd/bootstrap/authority.sdd

  Purpose: Determine what AI can and cannot do.
  Authority is a hard gate — actions outside authority are blocked.

  CAN: read-code, modify-code, run-tests, create-tasks, propose-skills
  CANNOT: change-global-rules, approve-production, modify-security-policy

  Outcome: AI knows its operational boundaries.

## Phase 3: MANDATORY RULES

  Read: .sdd/protocol/ROOT.sdd → .sdd/bootstrap/INDEX.sdd

  Purpose: Understand immutable system rules + rule priority hierarchy.

  Priority: 1. Correctness → 2. Security → 3. Architecture → 4. KISS → 5. DRY → 6. YAGNI

  Outcome: AI knows what it CANNOT do, and what takes priority when rules conflict.

## Phase 4: PROJECT RESOLVE

  Read: .sdd/INDEX.sdd → .sdd/project/INDEX.sdd

  Purpose: Resolve the project identity and scope.
  Outcome: AI knows what project it is working on.

## Phase 5: INDEX RESOLVE

  Read: .sdd/INDEX.sdd

  Purpose: Understand the universal routing table.
  INDEX.sdd contains ReadOrder and Navigation for everything.
  AI MUST NOT skip this step.

  Navigation chain:
    AI_BOOTSTRAP → ROOT INDEX → PROJECT INDEX → DOMAIN INDEX → TASK INDEX → RELEVANT REFERENCES

  Outcome: AI knows the structure of the entire system.

## Phase 6: ARCHITECTURE LOAD

  Read: .sdd/architecture/INDEX.sdd (current version)

  Purpose: Understand the current system architecture and constraints.
  Outcome: AI knows the architecture it must work within.

## Phase 7: GLOSSARY RESOLVE

  Read: .sdd/glossary/INDEX.sdd (mandatory terms only)

  Purpose: Load canonical terminology to prevent naming conflicts.
  Domain-specific terms loaded on demand when domains are resolved.

  Mandatory terms loaded at bootstrap:
    TASK, SKILL, CONCEPT, DECISION, REFERENCE

  Outcome: AI uses consistent terminology.

## Phase 8: TASK RESOLVE

  Read: .sdd/tasks/INDEX.sdd (current active tasks)

  Purpose: Determine the current active task and its dependencies.
  Outcome: AI knows what it is supposed to do right now.

## Phase 9: KNOWLEDGE COMPILATION

  Load (in priority order via Context Compiler):

  P0: System rules (immutable protocols)
  P1: Active task
  P2: Architecture (current version)
  P3: Relevant skills (for current task)
  P4: Active decisions (affecting current task)
  P5: References (relevant to current task)

  Purpose: Compile the current engineering state.
  Outcome: AI has minimum sufficient context for execution.

## Phase 10: ASSUMPTION CHECK

  Read: .sdd/assumptions/INDEX.sdd (current task assumptions)

  Purpose: Identify untested assumptions and their risk levels.

  RISK LEVELS:
    LOW       — minimal impact if wrong
    MEDIUM    — moderate impact, needs eventual validation
    HIGH      — significant impact, validation required before critical task
    CRITICAL  — must be validated before ANY execution

  Outcome: AI knows what it does not know, and whether it can proceed.

## Phase 11: READINESS GATE

  Validate all gates:

  G1: Project resolved          ✓
  G2: Task resolved             ✓
  G3: Architecture resolved     ✓
  G4: Required skills resolved  ✓
  G5: Critical assumptions      ✓
  G6: Permissions valid         ✓
  G7: Capabilities resolved      ✓
  G8: Resource locks available   ✓

  If any gate fails:
    STATUS: NOT_READY → AI does NOT execute

  If all gates pass:
    STATUS: READY → proceed to execution

  Outcome: AI has a binary readiness signal.

## Phase 12: CONTEXT COMPILE

  Read: .sdd/system/context/INDEX.sdd

  Purpose: Context Compiler transforms .sdd files into AI-executable context
  using progressive disclosure.

  Progressive Disclosure Levels:
    LEVEL 0: metadata     (always loaded)
    LEVEL 1: summary       (standard context)
    LEVEL 2: rules          (task-specific)
    LEVEL 3: implementation (on-demand)
    LEVEL 4: evidence       (review/release modes)

  Context Manifest is generated:
    loaded:    [list of nodes loaded]
    excluded:  [list of nodes excluded]
    reason:    [why each excluded node was excluded]

  Outcome: AI receives compiled context, not raw files.

## Phase 13: EXECUTE

   AI begins work following loaded context.

   AI MUST:
     - Stay within authority boundaries
     - Follow mandatory rules priority
     - Record discoveries
     - Use correct glossary terminology
     - Create assumptions for unknowns

   Mode-specific behavior:
     BOOTSTRAP_IMPL      → focuses on P1-P3 context
     BOOTSTRAP_REVIEW    → focuses on P4-P5 context + rules
     BOOTSTRAP_RESEARCH  → focuses on P5-P6 context + glossary
     BOOTSTRAP_RELEASE   → full context + security + approvals
     BOOTSTRAP_GOVERN    → loads governance/ capabilities + permissions + locks

## Phase 145: DESIGN CONTEXT DETECTION & AUTO-INVOCATION

   Purpose:
     Automatically invoke design skills when design-related work is detected,
     without requiring manual /sdd-design trigger.

   Context Detection:
     Analyze task description, file paths, and tool inputs for domain keywords.

     Design keywords:
       frontend, react, vue, angular, ui, ux, component, style,
       design, visual, taste, aesthetic, accessibility, mobile, screen

   Auto-Invoke Rules:
     [BOOT-145-01] Auto-invoke when design keyword confidence >= 80%.
     [BOOT-145-02] Auto-invoke MUST NOT block execution.
     [BOOT-145-03] Auto-invoke results are advisory, not blocking.
     [BOOT-145-04] Manual override via /sdd-design --manual.

   Flow:
     1. Analyze task context for design keywords
     2. If match confidence >= 80%:
        → Load .sdd/skills/design/INDEX.sdd
        → Run design evaluation
        → Attach findings to task context
     3. If no match: proceed without design skill
     4. Human MAY override with /sdd-design --manual

   Skill Selection:
     - FrontEnd task → visual-design + design-system + accessibility
     - Mobile task → visual-design + ux-patterns + accessibility
     - Design task → taste-evaluation + design-heuristics + design-review
     - General UI → design-system + accessibility

   Outcome:
     Design context is compiled before execution when relevant.
     No manual trigger required for standard design workflows.

## Discovery & Handoff

  During execution, AI may discover new information.
  Discovery is recorded in the execution journal.

  When handing off to another agent:
    - AI produces a HANDOFF manifest (not conversation history)
    - Handoff contains: completed work, changed files, discoveries,
      new unknowns, pending work, proof
    - Next agent bootstraps from handoff (not from conversation)

  See: @handoff.sdd

## Bootstrap Rules

  [BOOT-01] AI MUST follow this exact sequence — no skipping phases.
  [BOOT-02] AI MUST NOT read entire .sdd/ — use INDEX.sdd routing only.
  [BOOT-03] AI MUST resolve references before reading targets.
  [BOOT-04] AI MUST present compiled state before beginning work.
  [BOOT-05] AI MUST flag any UNRESOLVED_QUERY rather than guessing.
  [BOOT-06] AI MUST record bootstrap state for audit purposes.
  [BOOT-07] AI MUST check authority before any executing action.
  [BOOT-08] AI MUST validate readiness gates before execution.
  [BOOT-09] AI MUST use glossary canonical terms for all references.
  [BOOT-10] AI MUST produce handoff manifests, not conversation history.
  [BOOT-11] AI MUST resolve capability chain before any tool invocation.
  [BOOT-12] AI MUST check resource locks before executing.

## Never Assume

  IF information is missing:
    → do not invent
  IF architecture is unclear:
    → inspect architecture index
  IF requirement is ambiguous:
    → create assumption
  IF assumption is high-risk:
    → research / ask / block

Navigation:
